course: everyday
lesson: 314
title: Too-good-to-be-true offers, online scams and checking the news
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: If an offer sounds too good to be true, it almost always is. Free game coins, quick riches, and designer trainers for a tiny price are common tricks to steal accounts, money or card details.
explain: The most dangerous trick of all is a stranger offering sweets, a puppy or a treat to get a child to come with them. Never go anywhere with someone you do not know; run to a safe adult.
explain: Scammers copy real things with tiny changes: a fake celebrity account with a similar name, or a web address like «amaz0n» with a zero instead of an «o».
explain: Not everything you read or watch is true. Headlines are written to make you click, videos and voices can be faked, satire sites invent news as jokes, and bots repeat a message to make it look popular.
explain: A good checker asks: is this a fact that can be checked, or someone's opinion? Who else reports it? What sources does it give?
fact: Never use «generators» or give your password for free Robux, V-Bucks or game coins; they are scams that steal accounts or install harmful software.
fact: Real money takes real work; «get rich quick» offers are almost always tricks, so ignore them and tell an adult.
fact: Fake shops copy brand names and steal card details; a very low price on a strange website is a warning sign.
fact: Never go anywhere with someone you do not know, even if they offer sweets or a puppy; offers of treats are a known trick to lure children.
fact: Scammers make fake celebrity accounts with similar names, so check the real account and never send anything.
fact: Fake websites copy real ones with tiny spelling changes, so check the address letter by letter.
fact: Headlines are written to make you click and can be misleading, so read beyond them and check other sources.
fact: Videos and voices can be faked with technology (deepfakes); check what trusted news sources say.
fact: Satire is made-up news written as a joke, like The Onion; it can fool people who do not know it is a joke.
fact: A fact can be checked; an opinion is what someone thinks, and opinions should not be passed off as facts.
fact: Anyone can edit Wikipedia, so use it to start and check the references at the bottom.
fact: Some accounts are bots or fake accounts that repeat the same message to make an idea look more popular than it is.
exercise: A website promises 10,000 free Robux if you type in your username and password. What is it really? | A scam to steal your account or install harmful software | A thank-you gift from the game makers to their most loyal players | A reward for being a loyal player | A competition anyone can win fairly
exercise: Someone online tells a boy in Arizona he can earn hundreds of dollars a day from home, easily. What should he do? | Ignore it and tell an adult, because it is almost certainly a trick | Send his details to find out more | Ask his friends to join so they all get rich | Pay the small starting fee to try it, since he can always stop later if it does not work
exercise: Trainers that cost $150 in shops are sold for $15 on a website you have never heard of. What is wise? | Ask a parent to check, because it may be fake or never arrive | Buy them quickly before they sell out, because a sale like that never lasts | Use a parent's card without asking, it's cheap | Buy two pairs, since the price is so low
exercise: A friendly stranger in a car says, «Come and see my new puppy, I'll give you sweets.» What should a child do? | Refuse, get away and run to a safe adult | Go quickly with him, then come straight back | Go along if the stranger seems kind and calm | Ask the stranger to bring the puppy closer
exercise: A message says it is from a famous YouTuber giving away $500 to the first fans who send a small fee. What should you do? | Check the real account and do not send anything | Send the small fee quickly, so you are one of the very first fans | Share it with friends so they can win too | Reply with your address for the prize
exercise: A login page looks exactly like your game's, but the address says «r0blox.com». What is the warning sign? | A tiny spelling change in the address | The colours on the page look a bit too bright | The page loaded very fast | It asks for your username
exercise: A headline shouts «SCHOOLS TO BAN HOMEWORK FOREVER!». What should you do before believing it? | Read beyond the headline and check other sources | Believe it, because news in capital letters is always the most important | Share it so others know first | Stop doing homework straight away
exercise: A video shows a famous footballer saying he is quitting to become a dentist. His voice sounds a bit odd. What is wise? | Remember videos can be faked and check trusted news sources | Believe it, because videos are always real and can't be made up by computers | Tell everyone at school at once | Write him a letter to ask why
exercise: A story says scientists have taught pigeons to run a bank. It comes from a site known for funny made-up news. What is it? | Satire: made-up news written as a joke | A real science report | A secret the government is hiding from ordinary people | A mistake by a real news reporter
exercise: «Pizza is the best food in the world.» Is this a fact or an opinion? | An opinion, because it is what someone thinks | A fact, because many people agree | A fact, because pizza is popular | Neither, because it is about food
exercise: How can you tell a fact from an opinion? | A fact can be checked; an opinion is what someone thinks | A fact is said loudly; an opinion quietly | A fact is longer than an opinion | A fact comes from grown-ups and books; an opinion comes from children
exercise: A girl in Toronto uses a Wikipedia article for her homework. What should she do? | Use it to start and check the references at the bottom | Copy it all into her homework, because Wikipedia is always right | Never use it, because it is all made up | Change it so it matches her own ideas
exercise: Hundreds of accounts with no photos post exactly the same message. What could be happening? | They may be bots making an idea look more popular than it is | Lots of real people had the very same idea at once and wanted to share it | The message must be true if so many say it | The app copied one person's message by mistake
test: A pop-up says «Congratulations! You won free V-Bucks! Enter your password to claim.» Which is TRUE? | Real games never need your password to give you a prize | It is safe, because the game says congratulations and shows its own logo | It is safe if you only do it once | You should enter it quickly before it expires
test: A man at the park in Florida says he has lost his kitten and asks a girl to help him look for it in his car. What should she do? | Say no, move away and tell a safe adult at once | Help, because the kitten might be hurt | Help, but only for five minutes | Help, but only if he seems friendly and gentle and has a photo of the kitten
test: Why do fake shops sell famous brands at very low prices? | To trick people into giving card details or paying for fakes | Because big brands quietly give away their old stock online | Because they have lower rent than shops | Because it is the end of the season
test: Which website address is most likely a fake copy? | «paypa1.com» with the number one instead of an «l» | «bbc.co.uk» spelled correctly, ending in .co.uk | «nasa.gov» spelled correctly | «lego.com» spelled correctly
test: Your cousin sends you a clip of the Prime Minister singing a rude song. What is the best first step? | Check trusted news sources, because it could be a deepfake | Share it with everyone, because a video is real proof of what happened | Believe it, because the voice sounds right | Delete your cousin from your contacts
test: Which sentence is a fact, not an opinion? | Water boils at 100 °C at sea level | Summer is the best season of the whole year | Cats are nicer pets than dogs or rabbits | Football is the most exciting sport
test: Why can a satire story fool some people? | They do not realise it is a made-up joke | Satire sites are secretly run by the government | Satire is always printed in capital letters | Satire stories are checked like real news
test: An idea seems to be everywhere online because the same post appears thousands of times. What should you keep in mind? | Bots can make an idea look more popular than it really is | Anything posted thousands of times must be true, or someone would have stopped it | Only real people can post online | Popular posts are checked by experts
test: A boy finds a fact on Wikipedia with no reference after it. What is wise? | Check it in another trusted source before relying on it | Use it, because Wikipedia articles cannot be edited by just anybody | Add a made-up reference himself | Trust it more because it has no reference
test: guy on game says get rich quick, just send $5 | Ignore it and tell an adult | Send it, $5 is only a small amount | Send it, it pays you back $50 later | Ask him to prove it works first
test: taylor swift account dmd me, wants a gift card | It's a fake account, never send anything | Send it, she's raising money | Send a small one to check | Ask for a photo first
test: amazom.com — looks legit right? | No, check the address letter by letter, it's fake | Yes, it has the right logo and colours, so it's real | Yes, one letter doesn't matter | Only if the prices are low
test: 500 accounts saying the same thing so it must be true | Not always, bots repeat things to look popular | Yes, that many people can't all be wrong at once | Yes, if they all have photos | Only if they use capitals
explain: A boy in Canada typed his game password into a «free V-Bucks generator»; his account was stolen and years of items were gone in minutes.
explain: A girl in England found trainers for a tenth of the normal price on a strange site; her mum paid, the shoes never came and the card was used for other shopping.
fact: Free game-money generators are tricks to steal accounts and passwords.
fact: A price that is far too low on an unknown site often means a fake shop.
test: site says free robux just type ur password | Never; it's a trick to steal your account | Type it, it's free | Type an old password | Type it and change it later
test: designer hoodie £5 on some random site, buy?? | No, too cheap usually means a fake shop | Yes, what a bargain | Yes, if it has nice photos | Yes, if mum's card is handy
test: video of the head teacher saying school's cancelled, looks real | Check with the school; videos can be faked | Believe it, it's video | Share it with everyone | Stay home tomorrow without asking anyone
test: did u hear a kid sent his game login to a "famous YouTuber" account for free skins and "lost everything". lesson? | Scammers fake celebrity accounts; never send anything | Real YouTubers always give free skins | Only send your login to verified-looking accounts | Send it but change it right after
test: my mate says some site sells "real designer trainers for 5 quid, his cousin got 10 pairs". what's likely | A price that low on a strange site usually means a fake shop | It's a real bargain, buy them fast before his cousin buys the rest | Designer brands sell cheap online | His cousin must be rich now
test: everyone's sharing a funny article that the head teacher "banned homework forever", from a joke news site. pass it on as true? | No, it's satire; don't share it as if it were real | Yes, it's on a news site so it's true | Yes, everyone will be happy | Yes, and add your own bits
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises satire such as that of The Onion? | Invented news written as a joke, which may deceive those unaware of it | Factual news that has been carefully verified by professional journalists before publication | A form of advertising for newspapers | Government announcements in humorous form
exam: Identify the principal purpose of online «generators» promising free game currency. | To steal accounts and passwords | To reward loyal players with free gifts | To test the speed of the internet | To advertise official game updates
exam: It is given that a well-known brand is offered at a price far too low upon an unfamiliar website. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | A parent shall be asked to check, since it may be a fake shop | The item shall be purchased promptly, before the offer comes to an end | The card details shall be entered to reserve the item | The website shall be shared with friends
exam: State the purpose of accounts known as bots that repeat the same message. | To make an idea appear more popular than it is | To correct errors in the news faster than people could | To deliver messages much faster than humans can | To protect users from scams and from harmful links
